<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The =C2=A34.4bn cost of living support pac=
kage announced in John Healey=E2=80=99s budget yesterday is =E2=80=9Cbadly =
targeted=E2=80=9D, which means that even families receiving help face highe=
r energy bills than during the last energy price shock, according to the Re=
solution Foundation.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The poorest half of households will gain =
=C2=A3158, on average, from support measures this year and next =E2=80=93 o=
nly =C2=A314 more than the =C2=A3144 received by the richest half =E2=80=93=
 highlighting a =E2=80=9Ccomplete failure to target help at those who need =
it most=E2=80=9D, <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/=
u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRwPJJNAqeb5tNjqB_s2MiU3FTS9vZMRlnnRNNrmuQeKkEWliBo=
QLkr2yggDDAfxaum9rbNjwTg5hngY240qbiPRWxlOzBaJk3dXLBEWEWd30ptP23L8dYiKXA6aPA=
gQrTv1hQ0fHUPo0RGp6X9NWD6xgGX9kR_BjrXNcWkLG00AOe9YwQ0coLqZXDWKBf-EabPSmfoEY=
aFf2CnIOLpGBKUWmeB0UYjHMKpyZfTJvgWNSq5kILk38ZsWS8VLEoh5dUtiQPIdCfgQeJro36FV=
D934V2ncPNjAqvqpaeXGN6S_Q/4va/OhZIqPpIRP6X-15gpzJdGQ/h30/h001.Tq6vCAcWOxGSN=
tB3OeKGOb6NDvC1iYfk0VsGJsGlgjY" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">the thinktank sai=
d</a>.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Typical energy bills net of support are fo=
recast to be =C2=A31,750 this year (2026-27) =E2=80=93 higher in real terms=
 than during the last energy price shock (2022-23) when net bills were =C2=
=A31,685.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The chancellor raised taxes on banks and l=
andlords to fund a =C2=A3100 winter handout for 20 million households as of=
ficial forecasts made clear there is no end in sight for the cost of living=
 crisis.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Resolution Foundation=E2=80=99s analys=
is found that UK tax rises stand out globally. The UK=E2=80=99s internation=
ally remarkable consolidation in its public finances, touted by the chancel=
lor in his budget speech, is entirely due to a rising tax take during the c=
urrent parliament. The =C2=A376bn improvement in the UK=E2=80=99s primary b=
alance =E2=80=93 the gap between receipts and non-interest spending =E2=80=
=93 between 2023-24 and 2028-29 is more than fully accounted for by =C2=A31=
07bn of higher tax revenues.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Whitehall gets a bigger boost than househo=
lds. The budget included a =C2=A39bn top-up to departmental budgets over th=
e current spending review period =E2=80=93 twice as much as the =C2=A34.4bn=
 cost of living support package.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Past gain, future pain for living standard=
s. Median real annual disposable incomes for working age households are set=
 to grow by a paltry =C2=A3600 over the course of the parliament. Worse sti=
ll, those gains are already behind us. Between now (2026-27) and the end of=
 the parliament (2028-29), typical incomes are forecast to fall by =C2=A31,=
050 (3%).</p>
